lear favorite. A margin near -4 balances the tight surveys against the fundamentals and market pricing.", "watch": "Whether post-debate polling and outside spending confirm the race is genuinely a one-point contest or the surveys were overstating Andrews's support.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Darline Graham (R) is running."]}, "overview": "South Carolina's Senate race has tightened dramatically. Incumbent Darline Graham, an interim appointee, leads challenger Annie Andrews by only one to three points in recent surveys, a striking result in a state that leans Republican by eight points. Andrews has dramatically out-raised Graham, $10.8 million to under $400,000, though outside groups have spent heavily to support Graham.\n\nDespite the close polls, prediction markets still give Andrews only a 12-17% chance of winning, and most expert ratings sit at Likely R, with several shifting toward Lean R in late September. The race appears competitive but remains Graham's to lose, with debates and turnout dynamics in the final weeks likely to determine how close the finish is.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1565, "margin": -5.15}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2122, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2115, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2143, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1474, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.2135, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.2708, "margin": -4.0}]}